<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Marj Ardabil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/730">730</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-khazar-wars">Arab–Khazar wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Marj <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> was fought in December 730 near Ardabil, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, between a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> army and the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. The Khazars destroyed the Arab army and killed its commander, al-Jarrah ibn Abdallah al-Hakami, and then raided deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s lands as far as northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. The defeat led the Umayyads to send larger armies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in the following years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-marj-ardabil.jpg" alt="Battle of Marj Ardabil" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CrniBombarder!!! · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sab_dalj1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 726 the Umayyad caliph removed al-Jarrah ibn Abdallah from his post as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and appointed Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik, a general with a reputation for effectiveness. Maslama campaigned successfully against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> but failed to halt their raids altogether, and in 729 the caliph removed him and reappointed al-Jarrah. In 730 the Khazars launched a counterattack. An army of 30,000 men under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, Barjik, advanced to the town of Warathan and laid siege to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Al-Jarrah marched to relieve Warathan but failed to dislodge the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. As his army was outnumbered, a local man advised him to fall back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> and take up a defensive position on Mount Sabalan. He chose instead to give battle on the plain of Ardabil. His army was in a weak position, many of its troops being irregulars and levies, while the Khazars were in high morale.</p><p>The fighting began on 7 December, and the next two days were fierce and bloody. By the end of the second day the greater numbers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> had told, and most of the Arab veterans had been killed. That night the surviving levies escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, leaving al-Jarrah with few men. At dawn on the third day the Khazars attacked again. The remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> chose to fight to the death; al-Jarrah was killed and beheaded, and the Khazars took a great amount of booty from his camp. His army had numbered 25,000, and Agapius of Hierapolis reports that the Arabs lost 20,000 dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> followed their victory by attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> itself. They captured and sacked the town, massacred its men and enslaved its women and children, around 40,000 civilians in all. They went on to ravage the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><p>The disaster led Caliph Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik to appoint Sa'id ibn Amr al-Harashi to meet the threat, and he gave him 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> to recruit men from across the caliphate. Sa'id marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, intercepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, drove them out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and Azerbaijan and rescued some of the prisoners they had taken.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Marj Ardabil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Arab–Khazar wars of the seventh and eighth centuries the Khazars fought the Umayyad Caliphate across the Caucasus and destroyed an Arab army at <b>Marj Ardabil</b> in 730.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
